You can easily draw a chessboard from a FEN (Forsyth-Edwards Notation) string in LaTeX using the `xskak` package. This package is powerful and specifically designed for typesetting chess games and diagrams.

Here's a complete LaTeX code example that demonstrates how to do this, including multiple examples and explanations.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc} % Required for various characters, though not strictly for FEN
\usepackage{xskak}          % The primary package for chess diagrams
\usepackage{caption}        % For better captions in figures (optional but good practice)
\usepackage{geometry}       % For adjusting page margins (optional)
\geometry{a4paper, margin=1in} % Set margins for better presentation

\title{Drawing Chessboards from FEN in \LaTeX}
\author{AI Assistant}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section*{Introduction}
This document demonstrates how to draw a chessboard in \LaTeX using a FEN (Forsyth-Edwards Notation) string. The \texttt{xskak} package is the key tool for this purpose. It allows you to specify a FEN string and then render the corresponding board with pieces, coordinates, and various customization options.

\subsection*{Required Packages}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{xskak}: The core package for chess diagrams. It handles parsing the FEN and drawing the board.
    \item \texttt{caption}: (Optional but recommended) For customizing the appearance of figure captions.
    \item \texttt{geometry}: (Optional) For setting page margins.
\end{itemize}

\section*{How to Draw a Chessboard}

The general workflow is:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Include the \texttt{xskak} package in your preamble.
    \item Use the \texttt{\string\setchessboard} command to define global options for your boards (like size, coordinates, piece style).
    \item Use the \texttt{\string\chessboard} command, passing the FEN string as an option (or having set it previously with \texttt{\string\setchessboard\{fen=...\}}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Example 1: Initial Chess Position}
Let's start with the standard initial position of a chessboard.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    % Global settings for the chessboard environment
    % These settings will apply to all subsequent \chessboard commands
    % unless overridden for a specific instance.
    \setchessboard{
        maxfieldsize=10mm,     % Adjusts the size of the squares (e.g., 8mm, 12mm)
        showcoordinates=true,  % Shows algebraic coordinates (a-h, 1-8)
        % showmover=false,     % Don't show "White to play" / "Black to play" text below the board
        % showinfo=false,      % Don't show castling rights, en passant target, etc.
        piecefont=merida       % Choose a piece font: merida, skaknew, alpha, leipzig, berlin, etc.
    }
    % Draw the board using the FEN string
    \chessboard{fen=rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1}
    \caption{Initial Chess Position (FEN: \texttt{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1})}
    \label{fig:initial_position}
\end{figure}

\subsection*{Example 2: A Middle Game Position}
Here's a common position after the first few moves of the Ruy Lopez opening.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    % The settings from \setchessboard above are still active.
    % We just provide a new FEN string.
    \chessboard{fen=r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/4P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 4}
    \caption{Middle Game Position (FEN: \texttt{r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/4P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 4})}
    \label{fig:middle_game}
\end{figure}

\subsection*{Example 3: An Endgame Position with Custom Settings}
You can also pass options directly to the `\chessboard` command for a specific instance, overriding the global `\setchessboard` settings temporarily. Here we change the board size and piece font for this one diagram.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \chessboard[
        maxfieldsize=12mm, % Make it slightly larger for this one diagram
        piecefont=alpha,   % Use a different piece font just for this diagram
        showcoordinates=true,
        fen=8/8/8/8/8/8/7P/7K w - - 0 1 % FEN for King and Pawn endgame
    ]
    \caption{Simple Endgame Position (FEN: \texttt{8/8/8/8/8/8/7P/7K w - - 0 1}) with \texttt{alpha} pieces and larger squares.}
    \label{fig:endgame}
\end{figure}

\subsection*{Example 4: A Problem with Black to Move and Specific Castling Rights}
This example shows a position where black is to move, and only black's queenside castling is available.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \chessboard[
        maxfieldsize=9mm,
        piecefont=skaknew, % Another popular piece font
        showcoordinates=true,
        showmover=true, % Explicitly show whose turn it is
        showinfo=true,  % Show castling/en passant info
        fen=r3k2r/1ppn1ppp/p2bpq2/8/2BPP3/2N2N2/PP1B1PPP/R2QK2R b Qq - 0 7
    ]
    \caption{Middle Game Position (Black to move, FEN: \texttt{r3k2r/1ppn1ppp/p2bpq2/8/2BPP3/2N2N2/PP1B1PPP/R2QK2R b Qq - 0 7}) with \texttt{skaknew} pieces.}
    \label{fig:black_to_move}
\end{figure}

\section*{Understanding the FEN String}
A FEN string is a single line of text that describes the current state of a chessboard. It consists of six fields, separated by spaces:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Piece Placement (Rank 8 to 1):} Describes the position of pieces on the board, from rank 8 to rank 1. Each rank is separated by a forward slash (`/`).
          \begin{itemize}
              \item `p, n, b, r, q, k`: Black pawn, knight, bishop, rook, queen, king.
              \item `P, N, B, R, Q, K`: White pawn, knight, bishop, rook, queen, king.
              \item `1-8`: Numbers represent the count of empty squares. For example, `8` means an empty rank, `1P6` means a pawn on the second square, and six empty squares follow.
          \end{itemize}
          \quad Example: \texttt{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR}
    \item \textbf{Active Color:} Indicates whose turn it is to move.
          \begin{itemize}
              \item `w`: White to move.
              \item `b`: Black to move.
          \end{itemize}
          \quad Example: \texttt{w}
    \item \textbf{Castling Availability:} Indicates which castling moves are still possible.
          \begin{itemize}
              \item `-`: No castling is possible.
              \item `K`: White kingside castling is possible.
              \item `Q`: White queenside castling is possible.
              \item `k`: Black kingside castling is possible.
              \item `q`: Black queenside castling is possible.
              \item Combinations are possible, e.g., `KQkq`, `Qk`.
          \end{itemize}
          \quad Example: \texttt{KQkq}
    \item \textbf{En Passant Target Square:} Specifies the target square if an en passant capture is possible.
          \begin{itemize}
              \item `-`: No en passant capture is possible.
              \item A square name (e.g., `e3`, `d6`): Indicates the target square.
          \end{itemize}
          \quad Example: \texttt{-}
    \item \textbf{Halfmove Clock:} The number of halfmoves since the last pawn advance or capture. This is used for the fifty-move rule (a draw if no pawn moves or captures in 50 consecutive moves for each side).
          \quad Example: \texttt{0}
    \item \textbf{Fullmove Number:} The number of the full move. It starts at 1 and is incremented after Black's move.
          \quad Example: \texttt{1}
\end{enumerate}

\section*{Conclusion}
The \texttt{xskak} package provides a very convenient and powerful way to include chess diagrams in your \LaTeX{} documents. By simply providing a FEN string, you can generate accurate and customizable representations of any chessboard state.

\end{document}
